{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791566077,"nonce":"iK/BXi3u2qGuJllMQ4uBwks3sAE=","title":"display: none does not hide text: a hidden subtree's text draws one letter per line at the corner","message":"Found while checking what a signal-bound Display would need (2c565dc). Rendering div().hidden().child(text(\"HIDDEN div text\")) next to two visible labels, offscreen, draws the hidden text as a column of single letters at the top-left of the scene (\"d\", \"i\", \"v\", \"t\", \"e\", \"x\", \"t\"). The hidden div's own background is not drawn, because its box is zero-size; its text is, because text drawn into a zero-width box wraps every character and still lands on screen.\n\nCause: taffy gives a display: none node and its descendants a zero-size layout, and nothing in paint looked at the display. The paint walkers (basic, layout_renderer, motion) and blinc_app's text collection each skip a node only when props.visible is false, and only the CSS visibility property ever clears that. So .hidden() and CSS display: none left the subtree drawing.\n\nFixed by LayoutTree::is_display_none, checked beside props.visible at those four places. Pinned by blinc_app tests::a_display_none_subtree_draws_nothing: a scene with a hidden subtree must render the same pixels as the scene without it (178 pixels differed before).\n\nNote for a later look: the stylesheet maps CSS visibility: hidden to taffy display none (\"collapses element from layout\") while the paint comment says it preserves layout space. The two disagree about what visibility: hidden should do; not changed here.","files":null}]}